mat=header('03 / Matmul example','Matrix multiplication','Compare single-core, larger-block, and multicore matmul implementations.',[])
mat+=section('math','1. Matmul animation','<p>For A[64,128] and B[128,96], each output tile accumulates four products of 32×32 tiles; select an output and step through K.</p>'+'<div class="interactive"><div class="interactive-header"><h3>Trace one output tile</h3><span>64 × 128 @ 128 × 96</span></div><p>Select C, then advance through K. Highlighted A and B tiles feed the selected output.</p><div id="tile-demo"></div><div class="presets"><button id="tile-prev">← K</button><button id="tile-next">K →</button></div><div id="tile-equation" class="formula"></div></div>')
mat+=section('complete','2. Single core','<p>The submission platform supplies tiled BF16 tensors A[M,K], B[K,N], and out[M,N], with dimensions divisible by 32. One core computes one output tile at a time: the reader supplies input tiles, compute accumulates across K, and the writer stores the result.</p>'+code(kernel('matmul.py'),'Single core · matmul.py'))
mat+=section('blocking','3. Larger blocks','<p>Keep one core and set BM=BN=BK=2, so each block spans 64×64 values. The loop structure stays the same; larger slices reuse inputs across four output tiles. Use dimensions divisible by 64.</p>'+code(kernel('matmul_larger.py'),'Larger blocks on one core · matmul_larger.py'))
mat+=section('multicore','4. Multicore','<p>Keep the same block sizes and use <code>grid="full"</code>. Each core flattens its <code>(column, row)</code> coordinate into one index and owns a contiguous range of output blocks; each core runs its own reader, compute, and writer over the same assigned blocks. The <code>q &lt; mb * nb</code> guard skips unused assignments.</p>'+code(kernel('matmul_blocked.py'),'Larger blocks across cores · matmul_blocked.py'))
lesson('matmul','Matmul example','Animation, single-core code, larger blocks, and multicore code.',mat)
